{%- comment -%}
  ─── CATEGORY NAVIGATION ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  WHY THIS IS NOT A SHOPIFY MENU. Two reasons, both measured on the live shop.

  1. The app cannot build one. `read_online_store_navigation` and
     `write_online_store_navigation` are both ACCESS_DENIED for this app
     (probed 2026-08-15, see scripts/shopify/print-menu-plan.mjs), so a menu is
     hand-built in the admin and then drifts. The live Main menu today holds
     five rows — Home, New In, Sneakers, Caps & Hats, Under R500 — against a
     catalogue of sixteen categories. Eleven categories have no way in at all.

  2. The COLLECTIONS cannot be trusted as the source either. Measured
     2026-08-21 across all 703 live products:

         collection        on shop   should hold
         sneakers              117           324
         caps-hats               4            65
         tracksuits              0            64
         clothing                0            74
         bags                    0            34
         fragrance               0            17
         pants                   0            27
         jackets                 0            22
         hoodies-sweats          0            18
         t-shirts                0            14
         soccer-boots            0             2
         shorts                  0             4

     559 of 703 live products are in NO category collection. Navigation built
     from collections would show a shopper 117 of 324 sneakers and would not
     offer tracksuits at all — while 64 tracksuits are live and tracksuits are
     the SECOND best-selling category in the shop.

  SO THIS NAVIGATES BY TAG. Every live product carries exactly its category and
  subcategory as tags (verified: 703/703), and Shopify serves a real,
  server-rendered, paginated, crawlable page at /collections/all/{tag}. That
  URL is complete and correct today, needs no admin work, needs no data
  migration, and STAYS correct when new products are pushed. If the collections
  are ever repaired (`node scripts/shopify/sync-collections.mjs --commit`) these
  URLs keep working unchanged — there is nothing to migrate either way.

  EMPTY ROWS CANNOT APPEAR. `collections.all.all_tags` lists only tags that are
  on at least one published product, so a row is rendered only if the tag is in
  that list. A category that sells out entirely disappears from the navigation
  by itself, and comes back by itself. Nothing to maintain.

  THE TREE is grouped as the DATA groups it, not as a category tree was
  imagined: "Caps & Hats" sits under Clothing because every caps product carries
  the Clothing tag, so the Clothing parent page genuinely contains them. Two
  categories are deliberately absent:
    • "Clothing — Uncategorized" (5 products) — not a phrase to show a shopper.
      Those products still appear under New In, under Clothing, and in search.
    • "Accessories" as a parent row — the tag covers Bags but not Perfume, so a
      parent link would quietly exclude a category. Bags and Perfume are top
      rows of their own instead.

  ROW ORDER is POS sell-through, best first, from the shop's own tills over the
  56 days to 2026-08-21 (scripts/shopify/sell-through.mjs). Footwear leads at
  37.8% of units, Clothing follows.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
    WHY EVERY TAG LINK CARRIES ?sort_by=created-descending

    `/collections/all/{tag}` inherits the sort of `collections.all`, and that
    collection's default is `title-ascending` — measured on production
    2026-08-21: /collections/all opens on "AC Milan…", /collections/all/perfume
    on "Acquadi…", /collections/all/tracksuits-sets on "Central Cee…". All
    alphabetical.

    Alphabetical is the ONE ordering that is worthless here. These titles are
    machine-generated and every brand term is stripped out of them, so A-to-Z is
    a sort on noise — and because tag URLs ARE the category navigation, it was
    the landing order of every category in the shop.

    Newest-first is the right default for clearance stock, and it is the order
    the real collections already use. Stated as a parameter rather than assumed,
    because a shopper who arrives from a search engine gets whatever Shopify
    defaults to, and the sort control on the page says so honestly either way.
  {%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign all_tags = collections.all.all_tags

  # label ~ kind ~ target ~ optional children (comma-separated tag names)
  assign rows = 'New In~collection~new-in;Footwear~tag~Footwear~Sneakers,Boots,Sandals & Slides,Soccer Boots;Clothing~tag~Clothing~Tracksuits & Sets,Caps & Hats,Jerseys,Polos,Jeans & Denim,Jackets & Coats,Hoodies & Sweatshirts,T-Shirts,Shorts & Vests;Bags~tag~Bags;Perfume~tag~Perfume;Under R500~collection~under-r500' | split: ';'
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  ONE TREE, TWO SURFACES. `variant: 'drawer'` renders the same categories as
  Dawn's slide-out menu list instead of the top strip.

  This is why they cannot disagree: the hamburger used to render
  `section.settings.menu.links`, the hand-built Shopify Main menu, which held
  four rows while the shop had thirteen collections — and the app can never
  repair that, because it has no `write_online_store_navigation` scope. Reading
  the collections themselves means nobody has to touch the admin again and the
  two surfaces are the same data by construction rather than by discipline.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if variant == 'drawer' -%}
  <ul class="menu-drawer__menu has-submenu list-menu" role="list">
    <li>
      <a href="/collections/all?sort_by=created-descending"
         class="menu-drawer__menu-item list-menu__item link link--text focus-inset{% if request.path == '/collections/all' %} menu-drawer__menu-item--active{% endif %}">
        Everything
      </a>
    </li>
    {%- for row in rows -%}
      {%- liquid
        assign parts = row | split: '~'
        assign label = parts[0]
        assign kind = parts[1]
        assign target = parts[2]
        assign visible = false
        assign url = ''
        assign url_path = ''
        if kind == 'tag'
          if all_tags contains target
            assign visible = true
            assign url_path = target | handleize | prepend: '/collections/all/'
            assign url = url_path | append: '?sort_by=created-descending'
          endif
        else
          assign c = collections[target]
          if c != blank and c.all_products_count > 0
            assign visible = true
            assign url_path = c.url
            assign url = c.url
          endif
        endif
        assign kids = parts[3] | split: ','
      -%}
      {%- if visible -%}
        <li>
          {%- if kids.size > 0 -%}
            {%- comment -%}
              A native <details>. Dawn's own submenu JS is bound to its
              linklist markup, and native disclosure needs no script at all —
              which also means the drawer still opens and closes with
              JavaScript off.
            {%- endcomment -%}
            <details class="mc-drawer-group">
              {%- comment -%}
                Dawn sets `summary { list-style: none }`, which removes the
                browser's own disclosure triangle — so without a marker of our
                own, "Footwear" looked exactly like the plain links beside it
                and gave no hint that it opens.
              {%- endcomment -%}
              <summary class="menu-drawer__menu-item list-menu__item link link--text focus-inset mc-drawer-summary">
                {{ label }}
              </summary>
              <ul class="menu-drawer__menu list-menu" role="list">
                <li>
                  <a href="{{ url }}" class="menu-drawer__menu-item link link--text list-menu__item focus-inset">
                    All {{ label | downcase }}
                  </a>
                </li>
                {%- for kid in kids -%}
                  {%- if all_tags contains kid -%}
                    {%- assign kid_path = kid | handleize | prepend: '/collections/all/' -%}
                    {%- assign kid_url = kid_path | append: '?sort_by=created-descending' -%}
                    <li>
                      <a href="{{ kid_url }}"
                         class="menu-drawer__menu-item link link--text list-menu__item focus-inset{% if request.path == kid_path %} menu-drawer__menu-item--active{% endif %}">
                        {{ kid }}
                      </a>
                    </li>
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              </ul>
            </details>
          {%- else -%}
            <a href="{{ url }}"
               class="menu-drawer__menu-item list-menu__item link link--text focus-inset{% if request.path == url_path %} menu-drawer__menu-item--active{% endif %}">
              {{ label }}
            </a>
          {%- endif -%}
        </li>
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  </ul>
{%- else -%}
<nav class="mc-nav" aria-label="Categories">
  <div class="page-width">
    <div class="mc-nav__scroll">
      {%- comment -%}
        The sort belongs here MOST of all: `collections.all` is the collection
        whose title-ascending default causes the problem in the first place, and
        this is the first row in the navigation, on every page of the shop.
        Fixing the children and leaving the parent alphabetical was the bug one
        link to the left.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <a class="mc-nav__link"
         href="/collections/all?sort_by=created-descending"
         {% if request.path == '/collections/all' %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>Everything</a>

      {%- for row in rows -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign parts = row | split: '~'
          assign label = parts[0]
          assign kind = parts[1]
          assign target = parts[2]

          # ── Is there anything behind this row? ──────────────────────────────
          assign visible = false
          assign url = ''
          assign url_path = ''
          if kind == 'tag'
            if all_tags contains target
              assign visible = true
              assign url_path = target | handleize | prepend: '/collections/all/'
              assign url = url_path | append: '?sort_by=created-descending'
            endif
          else
            assign c = collections[target]
            if c != blank and c.all_products_count > 0
              assign visible = true
              assign url_path = c.url
              assign url = c.url
            endif
          endif
        -%}

        {%- if visible -%}
          {%- assign kids = parts[3] | split: ',' -%}
          {%- if kids.size > 0 -%}
            <div class="mc-nav__group" data-mc-navgroup>
              <button type="button" class="mc-nav__toggle" data-mc-navtoggle aria-expanded="false">
                {{ label }} &#9662;
              </button>
              <div class="mc-nav__panel" hidden data-mc-navpanel>
                <a class="mc-nav__link" href="{{ url }}">All {{ label | downcase }}</a>
                {%- for kid in kids -%}
                  {%- comment -%} Same test, per child: no empty child row. {%- endcomment -%}
                  {%- if all_tags contains kid -%}
                    {%- assign kid_path = kid | handleize | prepend: '/collections/all/' -%}
                    {%- assign kid_url = kid_path | append: '?sort_by=created-descending' -%}
                    <a class="mc-nav__link"
                       href="{{ kid_url }}"
                       {% if request.path == kid_path %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ kid }}</a>
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endfor -%}
              </div>
            </div>
          {%- else -%}
            <a class="mc-nav__link"
               href="{{ url }}"
               {% if request.path == url_path %}aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ label }}</a>
          {%- endif -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
</nav>
{%- endif -%}
